export const CARDS:TeachingCard[] = [{"id":"card-01","title":"Interchangeable strategies","text":"Did you know that in zero-sum games, min-max strategies are interchangeable, so to reach an equilibrium players need not coordinate their play?"},{"id":"card-02","title":"A game an algorithm can solve","text":"Did you know that min-max strategies can be found via linear programming, so an algorithm can solve the game in polynomial time?"},{"id":"card-03","title":"The first experiment","text":"Did you know that in the first lab experiment of what became known as the Prisoner's Dilemma, participants cooperated in spite of Nash’s equilibrium prediction?"},{"id":"card-04","title":"Beyond independent strategies","text":"Did you know that many game theorists are sceptical about Nash’s assumption of independent strategies? Roger Myerson for instance remarked that, if intelligent life exists on other planets, then on most of them correlated equilibrium would probably have been discovered before Nash equilibrium."},{"id":"card-05","title":"An idea looking for a home","text":"Did you know that when John Nash formulated the Nash equilibrium in 1950, the Princeton economics faculty largely ignored it. Nash's ideas were instead embraced, debated, and developed almost entirely within the Princeton Mathematics Department under scholars such as Albert W. Tucker, Harold Kuhn and David Gale."},{"id":"card-06","title":"The risk we create","text":"Did you know that a mixed strategy in a game creates endogenous risk. So to mathematically solve a game using the minimax or Nash equilibrium concepts, economists must accept that players evaluate human-made probabilities using von Neumann Morgenstern expected utility and notably accept the independence axiom."},{"id":"card-07","title":"The independence axiom","text":"Do you know the exact content of the independence axiom? Holding a decision-maker’s action fixed throughout. If a decision-maker prefers opponent action A over opponent action B, they must automatically prefer an opponent strategy over A and C over an opponent strategy of equal weights over B and C."},{"id":"card-08","title":"The game theory revolution","text":"Did you know that the game theory revolution by which game theoretic modelling became embedded in applied fields of economics from the 1970s onwards could only happen once Reinhard Selten developed the subgame perfection for extensive-from games and John Harsanyi showed how to model incomplete information."},{"id":"card-09","title":"Matching students and schools","text":"Did you know that, beginning in New York, game theory helped design school matching algorithms around the world, including in London, as to eliminate incentives for parents to misrepresent their true preferences. Here is how it is done: Prospective students submit a private preference list, schools hold onto as many of their top-ranked students as they have slots, and ask the rejected students to apply elsewhere. In the next round, schools re-rank the students they held on to and new applicants, and once more hold onto as many of their top-ranked students as they have slots, and ask the rejected students to apply elsewhere. Notice: they can reject students held on to in a previous round. Rinse and repeat until no school has more applicants than slots."},{"id":"card-10","title":"Designing an auction","text":"Did you know that theorists’ preoccupation with auction theory helped design efficient allocation mechanisms across many public institutions, including for central bank lending, public procurement and the sale of public assets such as radio spectrum. Eric Maskin called auction design more an art than a science, but theorists helped with the design nonetheless and helped sharpen our attention which pitfalls to watch out for."},{"id":"card-11","title":"Strategy at the highest stakes","text":"Did you know that nuclear deterrence was the first big application of game theory? During the 1950s and 60s, strategists at the RAND Corporation used the Prisoner's Dilemma and Chicken models to justify the doctrine of Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD). A credible, devastating second-strike capability was argued to be the only available strategy to prevent a pre-emptive nuclear first strike."},{"id":"card-12","title":"A different view of bargaining","text":"Did you know that Nash first came up with his famous Nash bargaining solution when still a mathematics undergraduate at Carnegie Mellon? It was only when he arrived at Princeton that he realized that his old term paper was actually a major, missing piece of the game-theoretic puzzle. Today, the Nash Bargaining Solution changes how we interpret conflict. It shifts the focus away from the performative drama of negotiations—the bluffing, the yelling, and the table-thumping—and gives economists, diplomats, and strategists a clean vocabulary to identify who actually holds the power in a room—and why."}];
